The smaller head has to come first in the merged list. After choosing it, the rest of the work is the same problem with one node removed.

If either list is empty, the other list is already in the correct order, so it can be returned as it is. Equal values go to `list1` here, but either choice keeps the result sorted.

```java
class Solution {
    public ListNode mergeTwoLists(ListNode list1, ListNode list2) {
        if (list1 == null) {
            return list2;
        }
        if (list2 == null) {
            return list1;
        }

        if (list1.val <= list2.val) {
            list1.next = mergeTwoLists(list1.next, list2);
            return list1;
        } else {
            list2.next = mergeTwoLists(list1, list2.next);
            return list2;
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n + m)` because each node is handled once.
Space complexity: `O(n + m)` in the worst case for the recursive call stack.

An empty `list1`, an empty `list2`, or two empty lists are all covered by the first checks.

Instead of only moving a tail pointer, this version inserts nodes from `list2` into the right places in `list1`. `previous` stays behind `current`, which makes it possible to place a node before `current`.

This is still in-place and does not create result nodes. The small dummy node only protects the case where a node from `list2` must become the new head. The input links are changed.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public ListNode MergeTwoLists(ListNode list1, ListNode list2) {
        if (list1 == null) {
            return list2;
        }
        if (list2 == null) {
            return list1;
        }

        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0, list1);
        ListNode previous = dummy;
        ListNode current = list1;

        while (current != null && list2 != null) {
            if (current.val <= list2.val) {
                previous = current;
                current = current.next;
            } else {
                ListNode nextList2 = list2.next;
                previous.next = list2;
                list2.next = current;
                previous = list2;
                list2 = nextList2;
            }
        }

        previous.next = current != null ? current : list2;
        return dummy.next;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n + m)`, since each pointer moves forward through the lists.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

The early returns handle an empty list before any node values are read.

Both lists are already sorted, so the next node must be the smaller of the two current nodes. A dummy node gives the result list a fixed starting point, while `tail` moves as nodes are attached.

For example, with `1 -> 3` and `2 -> 4`, the comparisons choose `1`, then `2`, then the remaining `3 -> 4` can be linked directly. The input nodes are reused, so the original links are changed.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* mergeTwoLists(ListNode* list1, ListNode* list2) {
        ListNode dummy(0);
        ListNode* tail = &dummy;

        while (list1 != nullptr && list2 != nullptr) {
            if (list1->val <= list2->val) {
                tail->next = list1;
                list1 = list1->next;
            } else {
                tail->next = list2;
                list2 = list2->next;
            }
            tail = tail->next;
        }

        tail->next = (list1 != nullptr) ? list1 : list2;
        return dummy.next;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n + m)`, where `n` and `m` are the list lengths.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

## Intuition

Both linked lists are already sorted. So, at each step, we only need to compare the current nodes of the two lists and choose the smaller value.

The key idea is to **reuse the existing nodes** instead of creating new nodes. We first choose the smaller first node as the `head`, then use a `temp` pointer to build the merged list by connecting the smaller node at each step.

## Approach

1. If `list1` is empty, return `list2`.
2. If `list2` is empty, return `list1`.
3. Compare the first nodes of both lists and choose the smaller one as `head`.
4. Move the selected list pointer to its next node.
5. Use `temp` to keep track of the last node in the merged list.
6. While both lists have nodes:

   * If `list1->val` is smaller or equal, connect `list1` to `temp` and move `list1`.
   * Otherwise, connect `list2` to `temp` and move `list2`.
   * Move `temp` to the newly connected node.
7. When one list becomes empty, connect the remaining part of the other list directly to `temp`.
8. Return `head`.

